================================================================================
DATABASE MIGRATION NOTES  --  MongoDB has been replaced with PostgreSQL
================================================================================

Read this before you pull. The app no longer runs without a local PostgreSQL
server, and there are setup steps you have to do once by hand.

Everything in this folder is Alembic, the tool that versions our database
schema. Alembic tracks changes to TABLES, not to the rows inside them.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. WHAT CHANGED
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

MongoDB is gone. So are the in-memory lists.

  BEFORE                                AFTER
  ------                                -----
  pymongo + MongoClient                 SQLAlchemy 2.x ORM
  app/database.py                       app/db/session.py + app/db/base.py
  accounts_collection.find(...)         db.query(Account).filter(...)
  accounts_db = []   (in memory)        accounts table in PostgreSQL
  customers_db = []  (in memory)        customers table
  transactions_db = [] (in memory)      transactions table
  Branch class, never stored            branches table + staff table

Only accounts had ever been wired into Mongo. Customers, transactions and
branches were still Python lists that emptied every time the server
restarted, so those are now persisted for the first time.

Plain classes became SQLAlchemy models. The Module 1 object design survives:
inheritance, encapsulation and polymorphism all still work, and the business
methods (deposit, withdraw, get_balance, account_type, update_info,
deactivate, get_total_balance, staff_to_manager_ratio) are unchanged.

One thing did degrade. Account can no longer inherit from ABC, because
Python cannot combine ABCMeta with SQLAlchemy's declarative metaclass on the
same class. account_type() raises NotImplementedError instead, so a subclass
that forgets to implement it still fails loudly, just when the method is
called rather than when the object is built.

Merged in from branch 02-qays:
  - owner_id     required on every account, foreign key to customers.id
  - account_number  customer-facing number, starts at 1000

Note that 02-qays's owner_id and the customer_id column added during the
Postgres migration were the same idea. They were merged into ONE column
named owner_id. There is no customer_id column.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. FIRST-TIME SETUP  (do this once)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Step 1. Install PostgreSQL 18
   https://www.postgresql.org/download/windows/
   During the installer:
     - keep the default port 5432
     - remember the password you set for the "postgres" user
     - UNCHECK "Launch Stack Builder" on the last screen, we do not need it

Step 2. Install the Python packages
   pip install -r requirements.txt

Step 3. Create the database
   Open pgAdmin (installed with PostgreSQL) and create a database called
   bankingapp. Or from a terminal:

     "C:\Program Files\PostgreSQL\18\bin\psql.exe" -U postgres -h localhost
     CREATE DATABASE bankingapp;
     \q

Step 4. Make your .env file
   Copy .env.example to .env, then put your own postgres password in it.

     copy .env.example .env

   .env is in .gitignore and must stay that way. Do not commit your password.
   .env.example is the committed copy and holds a placeholder instead.

Step 5. Build the tables
   python -m alembic upgrade head

   This creates all five tables plus the account_number sequence. You do not
   write any CREATE TABLE statements yourself, the migrations do it.

Step 6. Run it
   python -m uvicorn app.main:app --reload

Use "python -m alembic", not bare "alembic". pip installs alembic.exe into a
Scripts folder that is usually not on PATH on Windows.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. EVERYDAY ALEMBIC COMMANDS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  python -m alembic upgrade head      apply every migration you do not have
  python -m alembic current           which migration your database is on
  python -m alembic history           list all migrations
  python -m alembic downgrade -1      undo the most recent migration

After you pull from git, run "upgrade head". If someone added a migration
and you skip this, your code will expect columns your database does not have
and you will get confusing errors.

CHANGED A MODEL? Generate a migration for it:

  python -m alembic revision --autogenerate -m "short description"

Then OPEN THE GENERATED FILE AND READ IT before running upgrade. Autogenerate
guesses, and it gets things wrong. See section 6.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. THE SCHEMA
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  branches        id, branch_code (unique), location, manager_id
  staff           id, name, branch_id -> branches.id
  customers       id, name, email, branch_id -> branches.id, is_active
  accounts        id, account_number (unique), owner, balance, type,
                  branch_id -> branches.id, owner_id -> customers.id
  transactions    id, transaction_type, amount, timestamp,
                  from_account_id -> accounts.id, to_account_id -> accounts.id

Things worth knowing:

  "type" on accounts is the discriminator column. It holds "Savings" or
  "Checking" and is how SQLAlchemy knows whether to build a SavingsAccount
  or a CheckingAccount when loading a row. It is named "type" and not
  "account_type" so it does not collide with the account_type() method.

  Money is numeric(12,2), not float. Floats cannot store 0.10 exactly and
  the error compounds. In Python this means balances come back as Decimal,
  not float, so the controllers call float() when building JSON responses.
  Maximum balance is 9,999,999,999.99.

  account_number comes from a PostgreSQL sequence starting at 1000. Module 1
  used a class counter, which restarted at 1000 every time the server did and
  would have handed the same number to two different accounts.

  All the ID counters that used to live in Python are gone for the same
  reason. PostgreSQL assigns ids now.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5. API CHANGES THAT WILL BREAK OLD REQUESTS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

POST /api/v1/accounts
  - owner_id is now REQUIRED. Leaving it out gives a 422.
  - the customer named by owner_id must already exist, or you get a 404.
  - branch_id is still optional, but if you send one the branch must exist.
  - the response now includes account_number and owner_id.

POST /api/v1/customers
  - branch_id is an INTEGER now, it used to be a string. Send 1, not "1".
    It had to change because it points at branches.id, and accounts had
    always used an integer.

GET /api/v1/transactions
  - this actually returns data now. It always returned an empty list before,
    because transactions lived in a list that was wiped on every restart.

There is NO branch endpoint yet. Branches can only be created by calling
app/services/branch_service.py directly. Since accounts and customers both
point at a branch, somebody needs to build branch_controller.py.

Transfers are now atomic. Both balance changes and the history row are
written in a single commit, and the two account rows are locked in ascending
id order while it happens. Under Mongo a crash halfway through a transfer
could leave money withdrawn but never deposited.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
6. GOTCHAS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

sqlalchemy.url in alembic.ini is deliberately blank.
  alembic.ini is committed to git, so a real connection string there would
  publish the database password. alembic/env.py reads DATABASE_URL out of
  .env instead and fills the value in at runtime. Leave the line empty.

Migration 76e20500de2e was edited by hand. Do not regenerate it.
  Autogenerate produced a column defaulting to nextval('account_number_seq')
  but never emitted the CREATE SEQUENCE, so the migration failed on the first
  run. The CreateSequence and DropSequence calls were added manually.
  Autogenerate also left both new constraints unnamed, which meant the
  downgrade had no name to drop them by, so they were named explicitly.
  The lesson: always read a generated migration before applying it.

Autogenerate cannot see a model that nobody imported.
  If you add a new model file, import it in app/models/__init__.py. Alembic
  compares your models against the database, and a model it never imported
  looks like a table that should be DROPPED.

Deleting rows does not reset the id sequence.
  Delete every account and the next one still gets the id after the highest
  one you deleted. This is normal, do not try to fix it.

MongoDB is still installed on machines that had it.
  Nothing references it any more. You can stop the service and drop the old
  bankingapp database whenever you like.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
7. WHERE THINGS LIVE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  app/db/base.py         Base class every model inherits from
  app/db/session.py      engine, SessionLocal, and the get_db() dependency
  app/models/            SQLAlchemy models (were plain classes)
  app/services/          query the database, each takes a db session
  app/controllers/       endpoints, get a session via Depends(get_db)
  alembic/versions/      the migrations themselves
  .env                   your real password, never committed
  .env.example           the template, committed

Services take a session as their FIRST argument now:

  BEFORE   create_account(owner, account_type, balance, branch_id)
  AFTER    create_account(db, owner, owner_id, account_type, balance, branch_id)

Controllers get that session from FastAPI:

  def open_account(request: AccountCreateRequest, db: Session = Depends(get_db)):

Services do not commit during a transfer. record_transfer() only stages its
row, and the controller commits once at the end. That is what makes the whole
transfer succeed or fail as one unit. Keep it that way.